course: dictionary
lesson: 9865
title: Words for tools and safety
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A syringe is a tube with a plunger, which is a rod that slides in and out. Pull the plunger back and the tube fills with liquid; push it in and the liquid comes out. «Liquid medicine is measured with a measuring syringe or spoon, because kitchen spoons are different sizes.» The clues are liquid, medicine and measured, so a syringe must be a tool for measuring liquid.
explain: Some syringes have a needle on the end, and nurses use these to give injections such as vaccines. Others have no needle at all. An oral syringe is one of these, and it gives an exact amount of medicine by mouth. Ask yourself: does the sentence mention a needle? If it does not, the syringe is probably the needle-free kind.
explain: «Never touch a needle or syringe you find; mark the spot and tell a grown-up, because needles can carry blood-borne diseases.» Words like never and tell a grown-up are warnings. They show that a found syringe is something to stay away from, and the safe thing to do is to get an adult straight away.
fact: A syringe is a tube with a plunger that sucks in liquid and pushes it out.
fact: «Syringe» can mean a tool with a needle for injections, or a tube without a needle for measuring medicine.
fact: In hospitals, «sealed plastic syringes» are kept free of germs and «used only once».
fact: In science, air can be squashed inside a syringe, but water cannot.
fact: If you find a syringe or needle outside, never touch it; tell a grown-up straight away.
exercise: «Liquid medicine is measured with a measuring syringe or spoon.» What is a syringe? | A kind of hat worn by nurses | A small, sharp animal | *A tube with a plunger that sucks up and pushes out liquid | A type of hospital bed
exercise: In hospitals, syringes are «used only once». What does this tell you? | They are used for cooking | They are kept for years and reused | *They are thrown away after one use | They are made of wood
exercise: «A boy in Sydney finds a syringe in the park.» What should he do? | Pick it up and take it home | Put it in his pocket for later | *Never touch it; tell a grown-up straight away | Use it to squirt water at his friends
test: In science, a syringe shows that air can be squashed, but what cannot? | Air | Plastic | *Water | Sand
test: What is an oral syringe used for? | Injecting vaccines into the arm | Pumping up bicycle tyres | *Giving an exact amount of liquid medicine by mouth | Cleaning windows
exam: Which phrase names a syringe with no needle, used by mouth? | dangerous syringe | needle and syringe | *oral syringe | syringe lamp
